Pages = {958-969},
Month = {AUG 9},
Abstract = {PurposeThis paper seeks to investigate whether women exhibit greater
   risk-aversion behavior than men in investments by estimating the
   influence of gender on portfolio
   volatility.Design/methodology/approachData on the volatility observed in
   the portfolio in the last six months, last twelve months and since the
   individual became a client at one of the largest financial institutions
   in Brazil - and in Latin America - that operates in the capital markets
   are used. In addition to the gender explanatory variable, socioeconomic
   variables such as age, marital status, suitability, residence in
   capitals and declared assets are controlled, and multiple linear
   regression models are controlled.FindingsThe results show that gender is
   statistically significant in all models estimated to explain the
   volatility of investment portfolios, saying that women are more risk
   averse than men.Originality/valueThese findings are useful for the
   scientific literature that investigates behavioral finance by bringing
   empirical evidence for Brazil.},
